            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Makes a tensor of random samples from the CHI-SQUARED distribution: the sum of the squares of several independent bell-curve samples.\n\nWith df 3 each sample is what you get by squaring three standard normal samples and adding them up - the squared length of a random 3-D vector. It is the distribution of a sum of squared errors, and the reference curve for chi-squared tests. Always positive; the mean is df.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.dist.chi2 <dim 0: int> <dim 1: int> ...\nWhole numbers give the shape of each batch of samples; with none\nthe node sends one sample at a time.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.dist.chi2 64 64\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin (button):\nThe small button at the top left of the node. Click it, or send it anything, for a fresh tensor of samples. Only this inlet makes the node send.\n\nshape (text, also an inlet):\nThe size of the tensor of samples, as whole numbers: [64, 64] is a 64 by 64 grid, [1000] a row of a thousand. Numbers typed after the node name set it (t.dist.chi2 64 64); with none it is [1], a single sample. A list sent into the inlet works too. A new shape does not send by itself - the next click uses it.\n\ndf (drag float, also an inlet):\nDegrees of freedom - how many squared samples are added. Must be above 0; it need not be a whole number. 1 and 2 pile up against 0; from 3 up there is a hump near df - 2 that moves out and becomes more bell-like. Default 1 on a new node; the demo sets 3.\n\nEach parameter is a drag float on the node and also an inlet. Setting one rebuilds the distribution but does NOT send; the next click samples from it. In the demo each slider goes through 't bang float', which passes the number to the parameter first and THEN clicks the node, so dragging a slider re-samples at once. A value the distribution cannot take (see each parameter) is refused with an error in the console, and the node goes on sampling with the last value that worked.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nrandom tensor:\nA float32 tensor on the cpu, in the shape set, filled with fresh independent samples on every click.\n\nTHE HISTOGRAM:\nt.histc divides the range from its min to its max into equal bins and counts how many of the 4096 samples land in each; the bar plot draws the counts, the first bin at the left. Its shape is the shape of the distribution. Samples outside min..max are not counted at all.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nload_bang draws the first 4096 samples when the page opens; click the node's own button for more. The heat_map (0 dark, 8 bright) is a speckle of mostly dark tones. The histogram starts at 0, humps at df - 2 and trails to the right; the mean is df. Drag df up and the hump moves right and widens.\n\nRELATED:\nt.dist.gamma is the general family this belongs to (rate 0.5, concentration df / 2). t.dist.fisher_snedecor is a ratio of two of these. t.dist.normal is what is being squared.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
